      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument next No. 02-1689, Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: There's a curious thing about what Dataflux did. Dataflux at one point moved to add the Mexicans as individuals as counterdefendants."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Grupo Dataflux (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BOYCE: There -- there was some confusion early on in terms of what exactly their status was and we, Dataflux, did not thoroughly explore that issue early on."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What was the date of Anderson? 1891?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Atlas",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Anderson v. Watt which, applying Mollan and applying Conolly, does specifically anchor it in the 1875 iteration of the diversity statute in the course of its discussion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They filed after the trial court dismissal and before the Fifth Circuit held in their favor, and I believe that the -- the second suit was filed in December of 2000 and dismissed in approximately October of 2001."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondent asks that this Court affirm the Fifth Circuit's judgment and hold that, based on this Court's precedent, the trial court had subject matter jurisdiction at the time of trial because the lack of complete diversity between the parties was cured before trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Greenberg",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "unlike a corporation, each partner's citizenship counts equally."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the Fifth Circuit rule allows that, I think Caterpillar allows that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Greenberg",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under rule 21, if they were dispensable, yes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And I suppose you would argue that if we're looking for bright line rules, we shouldn't make it turn on what the nature of a particular jurisdictional defect was or they moved to another State or you let one partner resign or something like that. The bright line rule, it seems to me, has to turn on whether it's equally bright line to say you can make this objection up till the time of a verdict in the trial court or it's equally bright line to say you can do it up to the time that the appellate court judgment is final. You can't do it after -- we all agree you can't do it on collateral attack."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1689",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "08e6a25ba0defcc0d153d411641a3aa72c22b60960a798adb847082cc474d898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-08e6a25ba0de-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "QUESTION: Wasn't the partnership sued as an entity? I thought that it was sued as a partnership."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
